description:
## Why

Parent acceptance criterion: 'Cache keys are correct (CRL: (issuer, distribution point); OCSP: (cert serial, issuer key hash, responder URL)).'

Real consumers refetch the same CRL/OCSP response many times within a short window; without caching, each path validation re-pulls from the network.

## What

Cache wrapper types that compose with `HttpCrlFetcher` and `HttpOcspFetcher`. Pluggable cache trait so consumers can swap the backing store (HashMap, moka, redis-backed, etc.).

API shape (Shape D, decided 2026-05-11 — see `--design` and comment thread for rejected alternatives):

```rust
pub trait RevocationCache: Send + Sync {
    fn get_crl(&self, key: &CrlCacheKey) -> Option<Arc<CachedCrl>>;
    fn put_crl(&self, key: CrlCacheKey, entry: CachedCrl);
    fn invalidate_crl(&self, key: &CrlCacheKey);

    fn get_ocsp(&self, key: &OcspCacheKey) -> Option<Arc<CachedOcspResponse>>;
    fn put_ocsp(&self, key: OcspCacheKey, entry: CachedOcspResponse);
    fn invalidate_ocsp(&self, key: &OcspCacheKey);
}

#[derive(Clone, Debug, PartialEq, Eq, Hash)]
pub struct CrlCacheKey {
    /// DN of the CRL issuer (cert issuer, OR cRLIssuer if indirect CRL).
    pub issuer_dn_der: Vec<u8>,
    /// CRL DP URI from the cert. `None` = "the" CRL for this issuer.
    /// `Some(uri)` for partitioned / multiple-DP cases (RFC 5280 §5).
    pub distribution_point_uri: Option<String>,
}

pub struct CachedCrl {
    /// RFC 5280 §5.2.3 monotonic version metadata — freshness comparator, NOT a key field.
    pub crl_number: Option<Vec<u8>>,
    pub this_update: SystemTime,
    pub next_update: Option<SystemTime>,
    pub bytes: Vec<u8>,
}

#[derive(Clone, Debug, PartialEq, Eq, Hash)]
pub struct OcspCacheKey {
    pub cert_serial: Vec<u8>,
    pub issuer_key_hash: Vec<u8>,
    pub responder_url: String,
}

pub struct CachedOcspResponse {
    pub produced_at: SystemTime,
    pub this_update: SystemTime,
    pub next_update: Option<SystemTime>,
    pub bytes: Vec<u8>,
}

pub struct InMemoryCache { /* HashMap-based + RwLock or DashMap */ }
impl RevocationCache for InMemoryCache { /* ... */ }

pub struct CachedHttpCrlFetcher<F, V, C> { /* wrapper */ }
pub struct CachedHttpOcspFetcher<F, V, C> { /* wrapper */ }
```

For CRL: extract CRLNumber + thisUpdate + nextUpdate from the parsed `CertificateList` (RFC 5280 §5.2.3 / §5.1.2.4 / §5.1.2.5) and store in `CachedCrl`. TTL based on nextUpdate.

For OCSP: parse the response just enough to extract producedAt + thisUpdate + nextUpdate; cache until nextUpdate (or producedAt + a configurable max-age fallback when nextUpdate is absent).

## Acceptance

- [ ] `CrlCacheKey` keys by `(issuer_dn_der, distribution_point_uri)` — both known a-priori to the wrapper.
- [ ] `OcspCacheKey` keys by `(cert_serial, issuer_key_hash, responder_url)` — all known a-priori to the wrapper.
- [ ] `CachedCrl` carries `crl_number` as freshness metadata (NOT a key field).
- [ ] `RevocationCache` trait is `Send + Sync`.
- [ ] `invalidate_crl` / `invalidate_ocsp` exist for explicit cache busts.
- [ ] `InMemoryCache` reference impl works.
- [ ] `CachedHttpCrlFetcher` and `CachedHttpOcspFetcher` delegate to underlying fetchers on miss; serve from cache on hit.
- [ ] TTL respected — expired entries return `None` from `get_*`.
- [ ] `CachedHttpCrlFetcher` compares fetched CRLNumber against cached CRLNumber on refresh and rejects rollback (newer is monotonic).
- [ ] Concurrent access safe (RwLock or DashMap).
- [ ] Unit tests: hit, miss, expiry, concurrent insert, invalidate, partitioned-CRL (`Some(uri)`) keying.
- [ ] Returns `Arc<CachedCrl>` / `Arc<CachedOcspResponse>` (not `Arc<Vec<u8>>`) so callers see freshness metadata without re-parsing.
- [ ] No persistence layer (in-memory only; documented).

## Out of scope

- Disk persistence (consumer's job; trait is the extension point).
- Cache size bounds (LRU/LFU eviction) — start with unbounded HashMap; consumer with a large fleet uses a moka-backed `RevocationCache` impl.
- Async cache variant — sync only per PKIX-a1yc.10.
- LDAP / file:// CRL distribution points — pkix-revocation-http is HTTP-only; non-HTTP fetchers will live in sibling crates and reuse the same trait.

## Effort

~250 lines + tests.

## Depends on

PKIX-a1yc.5 (HttpCrlFetcher), PKIX-a1yc.6 (HttpOcspFetcher).
